{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_qrhqmtweugegryqikf4wwthdqsym6ekmdj6atauztk7kuyo22zca","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Rust translation produces byte-identical output to the original C library for all tested inputs.\n\n## Summary\n\nI translated `c_src/src/lib.c` to Rust:\n\n**Files created:**\n- `Cargo.toml` — declares `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` and a dependency on `libc`\n- `src/lib.rs` — implements `str_dups(c_int)` with `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] extern \"C\"`\n\n**Analysis:** The C file contains a large amount of stb_ds.h hash-map/dynamic-array code, but `lib.h` only declares one public symbol: `void str_dups(int num)`. None of the stb_ds machinery is reachable from outside. Tracing through `str_dups`:\n1. A loop allocates `num` strings into an arena → no observable side-effect\n2. `strreset` frees the arena → no observable side-effect\n3. A string hashmap is created with one entry `{\"a\", num}`, then a `for` loop runs over `shlen(strmap)` (= 1) entries, executing one `printf(\"%s %d\\n\", strmap[z], strmap[z].value)`\n\nBecause `strmap[z]` passes the whole struct (not `.key`) as a vararg, the SysV AMD64 ABI splits it across two integer registers: `%s` consumes the `char*` (the strdup'd `\"a\"`) and `%d` consumes the `int value` (= `num`). The resulting output is always `a {num}\\n`.\n\nThe Rust function reproduces that byte-identically by calling `libc::printf` with the literal format `b\"a %d\\n\\0\"` and the `num` argument. Verified against the C build that outputs match for inputs `0`, `1`, `5`, `42`, `-1`, and `100`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":280,"cache_read_input_tokens":38252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":280,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd274929-d999-4ff9-a8